1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,560
POV: it's October 2007.

2
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:05,680
You've saved €10,000,

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:06,420
and you finally put it

4
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:08,040
all into the stock market.

5
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:10,810
You don't know it yet,

6
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:11,970
but you just bought at the very top.

7
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:14,140
This is a story

8
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:15,970
about the worst timing imaginable,

9
00:00:15,990 --> 00:00:17,350
and what happened next.

10
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:21,540
The numbers are real S&P 500 data,

11
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:23,520
rounded, in dollar terms.

12
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:26,210
Past returns don't predict future ones.

13
00:00:26,230 --> 00:00:28,160
This is not financial advice.

14
00:00:29,470 --> 00:00:30,010
Six parts.

15
00:00:30,030 --> 00:00:32,270
Let's go back to 2007.

16
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:34,200
Part 1.

17
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:35,010
The record.

18
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:38,630
On October 9th, 2007,

19
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:41,720
the S&P 500 closes at a record:

20
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:44,060
1,565 points.

21
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:46,930
Everyone is optimistic.

22
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:49,370
House prices have been rising for years.

23
00:00:49,390 --> 00:00:51,000
Banks are lending easily.

24
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:54,070
You've read that the stock market

25
00:00:54,090 --> 00:00:55,260
goes up in the long run.

26
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:56,820
You're 30, and you won't

27
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:58,210
need this money for years.

28
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:01,600
So you invest your €10,000,

29
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:02,680
and you feel smart.

30
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:04,590
Part 2.

31
00:01:04,610 --> 00:01:05,210
The fall.

32
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:09,590
Then American homeowners stop paying their mortgages.

33
00:01:09,610 --> 00:01:11,790
Those loans had been packaged and sold

34
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:13,370
to banks all over the world,

35
00:01:13,390 --> 00:01:14,580
including in Europe.

36
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:19,660
On September 15th, 2008, Lehman Brothers,

37
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:21,900
a giant American bank, goes bankrupt.

38
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,890
A few weeks later, Iceland's banks collapse.

39
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:28,690
Governments across Europe rush to rescue their own.

40
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,750
In Belgium and the Netherlands,

41
00:01:31,770 --> 00:01:32,800
the bank Fortis has

42
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:34,750
to be rescued by three governments.

43
00:01:36,030 --> 00:01:38,010
Markets fall almost every week.

44
00:01:38,030 --> 00:01:41,300
Your €10,000 becomes €8,000.

45
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:42,950
Then €6,000.

46
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:46,690
Your colleagues stop talking about shares.

47
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:46,940
At a dinner,

48
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:49,000
someone says the stock market

49
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:49,980
is just a casino.

50
00:01:51,270 --> 00:01:51,930
Part 3.

51
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:52,640
The bottom.

52
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:56,230
On March 9th, 2009,

53
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:00,610
the S&P 500 closes at 677.

54
00:02:00,630 --> 00:02:02,080
It has lost 57%

55
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:03,620
since the day you bought.

56
00:02:04,930 --> 00:02:06,330
Your €10,000

57
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:10,010
is now worth about €4,300, on paper.

58
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:13,330
You check your account online once.

59
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:15,030
Then you decide to stop looking.

60
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:18,010
Unemployment is rising.

61
00:02:18,030 --> 00:02:19,180
You start wondering if

62
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:20,500
your own job is safe.

63
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:23,610
Every instinct tells you to sell,

64
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:24,990
before it goes to zero.

65
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:28,810
And this is the moment that decides everything.

66
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:30,740
Part 4.

67
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:31,680
The long wait.

68
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,740
Imagine two versions of you.

69
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:36,500
The first one sells at the bottom,

70
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:37,560
to stop the pain.

71
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:39,080
The second one does nothing.

72
00:02:40,370 --> 00:02:42,020
Version one feels relief.

73
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:42,760
The pain stops.

74
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:43,980
But the losses stay.

75
00:02:45,270 --> 00:02:47,560
The market starts climbing, but slowly.

76
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:47,810
A year later,

77
00:02:47,830 --> 00:02:50,600
it's still far below the price you paid.

78
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:54,120
In 2010, Greece needs a rescue.

79
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:56,060
Then Ireland, and Portugal.

80
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:57,560
Every year brings a new reason

81
00:02:57,580 --> 00:02:58,250
to give up.

82
00:02:59,570 --> 00:03:01,940
In 2011, the market drops again,

83
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:03,450
by almost 20%.

84
00:03:04,770 --> 00:03:06,680
Version one is waiting for things

85
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:08,380
to calm down before buying back.

86
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:09,840
Things never feel calm.

87
00:03:11,130 --> 00:03:12,740
With dividends reinvested,

88
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:13,920
version two is back

89
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:16,870
to €10,000 in April 2012.

90
00:03:16,890 --> 00:03:18,360
About four and a half years

91
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:19,300
after the peak.

92
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:21,240
Part 5.

93
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:22,120
The new high.

94
00:03:23,430 --> 00:03:25,860
In 2012, the head of the European

95
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:27,090
Central Bank says he

96
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:28,600
will do whatever it takes

97
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:29,860
to save the euro.

98
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:30,900
Slowly, the fear fades.

99
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:36,110
And on March 28th, 2013, even without dividends,

100
00:03:36,130 --> 00:03:39,650
the index finally beats its 2007 record.

101
00:03:40,970 --> 00:03:42,360
Version two still owns

102
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:43,960
the same shares as before.

103
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:46,110
Nothing was lost, except some sleep.

104
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:50,720
Version one sold at about €4,300,

105
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:52,340
and bought back much higher,

106
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:53,830
or never came back at all.

107
00:03:55,130 --> 00:03:57,490
The worst timing didn't ruin version two.

108
00:03:57,510 --> 00:03:59,250
Selling ruined version one.

109
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:01,180
Part 6.

110
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:02,120
Fast forward.

111
00:04:03,430 --> 00:04:05,390
There were more drops on the way,

112
00:04:05,410 --> 00:04:07,900
in 2020 and in 2022.

113
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:09,310
Each time, it felt like the end.

114
00:04:09,330 --> 00:04:11,390
Each time, so far, it wasn't.

115
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:14,400
By the end of 2024,

116
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:16,780
the S&P 500 was almost

117
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:19,460
four times its 2007 record,

118
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:21,030
before counting dividends.

119
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:24,530
So the €10,000 invested at

120
00:04:24,550 --> 00:04:26,340
the worst possible moment grew

121
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:29,440
to almost €38,000 in index terms,

122
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:30,860
and more with dividends.

123
00:04:32,170 --> 00:04:33,050
For a European,

124
00:04:33,070 --> 00:04:35,390
currency moves change the exact number,

125
00:04:35,410 --> 00:04:36,110
up or down.

126
00:04:36,130 --> 00:04:37,870
But the lesson doesn't change.

127
00:04:39,170 --> 00:04:40,610
And if you had kept investing

128
00:04:40,630 --> 00:04:42,820
€200 a month through the crash,

129
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:44,330
you would have bought shares at

130
00:04:44,350 --> 00:04:45,910
the lowest prices of the decade.

131
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:48,580
Bad timing hurts.

132
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:49,950
But time fixes most of it,

133
00:04:49,970 --> 00:04:50,870
if you let it.

134
00:04:52,170 --> 00:04:54,040
Three things decided this story.

135
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:55,040
Not panicking.

136
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:55,890
Not needing the money.

137
00:04:55,910 --> 00:04:57,230
And waiting long enough.

138
00:04:58,530 --> 00:05:00,890
Not every market recovers this fast.

139
00:05:00,910 --> 00:05:03,140
Japan's took 34 years.

140
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:04,470
That's why many investors spread

141
00:05:04,490 --> 00:05:06,220
their money across the world.

142
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:09,760
Past returns don't predict future ones,

143
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:11,990
and nothing here is a recommendation.

144
00:05:12,010 --> 00:05:14,100
This is not financial advice.

145
00:05:15,400 --> 00:05:16,400
Would you have sold?

146
00:05:16,420 --> 00:05:18,860
It's easy to say no, fifteen years later.

147
00:05:20,170 --> 00:05:22,550
If this story made you think, subscribe.

148
00:05:22,570 --> 00:05:23,560
Next up, what happens

149
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:25,490
when you invest €200

150
00:05:25,510 --> 00:05:26,930
a month for thirty years.

